                "content": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the minimum side length $s$ of an equilateral triangle that can contain a unit square.\n\n## Setting Up the Problem\n\nPlace the equilateral triangle with vertices $A=(0,0)$, $B=(s,0)$, $C=(s/2, s\\sqrt{3}/2)$.\n\nA point $(x,y)$ is inside the triangle iff:\n- $y \\geq 0$ (above base)\n- $x \\geq y/\\sqrt{3}$ (right of left side $y = \\sqrt{3}\\,x$)\n- $x \\leq s - y/\\sqrt{3}$ (left of right side $y = \\sqrt{3}(s-x)$)\n\n## Key Structural Argument\n\n**The square must touch the base.** If the square's lowest point is at height $y_{\\min} > 0$, translating it downward decreases all $y$-coordinates. Since both slanted-side constraints become *easier* as $y$ decreases (the triangle widens below), this translation only tightens the base constraint. We can always lower the square until it touches the base without violating any other constraint.\n\n**The square must touch at least one slanted side.** Otherwise we could shrink $s$.\n\n## Parameterizing the Square\n\nWith the lowest vertex at $(a, 0)$ and rotation angle $\\theta \\in (0\u00b0, 90\u00b0)$, the vertices are:\n\n| Vertex | Coordinates |\n|--------|------------|\n| $P_1$ | $(a,\\; 0)$ |\n| $P_2$ | $(a+\\cos\\theta,\\; \\sin\\theta)$ |\n| $P_3$ | $(a+\\cos\\theta - \\sin\\theta,\\; \\sin\\theta+\\cos\\theta)$ |\n| $P_4$ | $(a-\\sin\\theta,\\; \\cos\\theta)$ |\n\n**Tightest left-side constraint** (from $P_4$):\n$$a \\geq \\sin\\theta + \\frac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sqrt{3}}$$\n\nSetting $a = \\sin\\theta + \\frac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sqrt{3}}$ (i.e., $P_4$ on the left side), the **tightest right-side constraint** gives:\n\n$$s \\geq \\frac{(\\sqrt{3}+1)(\\sin\\theta + \\cos\\theta)}{\\sqrt{3}}$$\n\nThis is valid for $\\theta \\in [30\u00b0, 60\u00b0]$. For $\\theta \\in [0\u00b0, 30\u00b0]$, the binding constraint from $P_3$'s right side gives a concave function whose minimum is also at the endpoints.\n\n## Minimizing Over $\\theta$\n\nSince $\\sin\\theta + \\cos\\theta = \\sqrt{2}\\sin(\\theta + 45\u00b0)$ is **maximized** at $\\theta = 45\u00b0$ and **minimized** at the endpoints of $[30\u00b0, 60\u00b0]$:\n\n$$s_{\\min} = \\frac{(\\sqrt{3}+1)}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{1+\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{(\\sqrt{3}+1)^2}{2\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{4+2\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}} = 1 + \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$$\n\n## Verification\n\nWith $s = 1 + \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$, place the **aligned** square centered on the symmetry axis:\n\n$$\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,0\\right),\\;\\left(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,0\\right),\\;\\left(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,1\\right),\\;\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,1\\right)$$\n\n- **Bottom vertices**: on the base $y=0$ \u2713  \n- **Top-left** $\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, 1\\right)$: left side gives $y = \\sqrt{3}\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} = 1$ \u2014 **on the boundary** \u2713  \n- **Top-right** $\\left(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, 1\\right)$: right side gives $y = \\sqrt{3}\\left(s - 1 - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) = \\sqrt{3}\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} = 1$ \u2014 **on the boundary** \u2713  \n\nAll four vertices lie on the boundary of the triangle. $\\blacksquare$\n\n## Result\n\n$$\\boxed{s = 1 + \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}}$$",
